// plan-batch.dot
//
// A PLANNER attractor: read ONE GitHub issue and converge on a plan.json
// that the deterministic goal-plan compiler ACCEPTS -- or abort honestly
// with a residual note and no plan.json at all.
//
// Runs inside a GitHub Actions job via the microsoft/amplifier-app-actions
// action. See pipelines/plan-batch/plan-batch.md for the full contract and
// the consuming workflow (.github/workflows/plan-batch.yml).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DESIGN ORDER (docs/primer.md sect.3 / docs/RUBRIC.md sect.0) -- sink, gate, loop,
// then work nodes:
//
//   1. SINK  -- a plan.json at the workspace root that
//               `python3 -m compiler plan.json -o /tmp/plan-check.dot`
//               exits 0 on. That command, run by a machine, is what proves
//               this pipeline is done. Nothing else counts.
//   2. GATE  -- CompilerGate: a deterministic shape=parallelogram tool node
//               that runs exactly that command and routes on its REAL exit
//               code plus the real existence of the artifacts it should have
//               produced. It never asks an LLM whether the plan is valid.
//   3. LOOP  -- rejection routes CompilerGate -> CheckAttempts -> RevisePlan
//               -> InstallPlan -> CompilerGate, carrying the compiler's own
//               named error as ONE curated line (not an append-only pile).
//               CheckAttempts is a real decision point with a cap; hitting
//               the cap routes to Abort (a residual note), never to a bare
//               fail and never to a shipped-anyway plan.
//   4. WORK  -- only then AuthorPlan / RevisePlan, the two LLM nodes.
//
// THREE-QUESTION TEST (docs/RUBRIC.md sect.1):
//   - Is there a cycle? Yes: InstallPlan -> CompilerGate -> CheckAttempts ->
//     RevisePlan -> InstallPlan, closed by loop_restart on RevisePlan's
//     forward edge.
//   - Is the exit gated on evidence? Yes: MarkAccepted is reachable only
//     through CompilerGate's `plan_ok` line, which requires compiler exit 0
//     AND a non-empty generated parent .dot AND a non-empty plan.json.
//   - Would it still land if an LLM node had a bad day? Yes. A malformed,
//     hallucinated, or entirely missing plan cannot reach MarkAccepted; it
//     loops with the compiler's named reason, and after the cap it aborts
//     with plan.json deliberately REMOVED so a bad plan cannot be shipped
//     downstream by a workflow step that only checks "did the job finish".
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RUNTIME CONTRACT (amplifier-app-actions -- verified, obey exactly)
//
//   INPUT: there is NO param channel. The only runtime-variable input that
//   reaches this graph is the built-in `$goal` substitution, which the action
//   sets to a formatted GitHub issue context block (event type, #number,
//   owner/repo, Title, Body, Labels, Author). Both LLM nodes consume `$goal`
//   directly; nothing else is parameterised. Where a knob would be useful
//   (the attempt cap) it is written as a shell default -- `${plan_max_attempts:-4}`
//   -- which resolves to 4 today and would pick up a real param for free if a
//   param channel ever appears. (Engine substitution leaves an unknown
//   `${key:-default}` token literal, so /bin/sh applies the default.)
//
//   CWD: the process cwd is $GITHUB_WORKSPACE (the checked-out repo).
//   Tool nodes (shape=parallelogram) resolve cwd via context.target_dir ->
//   graph.source_dir -> process cwd (docs/RUBRIC.md sect.3.8), which is NOT
//   guaranteed to be the workspace when this graph is fetched from a
//   git+https:// URL. Every tool node below therefore re-anchors explicitly
//   with `PB_WS=${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-$PWD}` and uses "$PB_WS/plan.json"
//   instead of trusting an inherited cwd.
//
//   LLM FILE WRITES: agent (shape=box) nodes have an unreliable cwd, so
//   neither LLM node is ever asked to write a RELATIVE path. They write to
//   the fixed ABSOLUTE path /tmp/plan-batch/plan.json, which is
//   cwd-independent, and the engine's `must_write=` fail-closed artifact
//   contract asserts the write actually happened (existence + freshness +
//   non-trivial content) rather than trusting the node's own success line
//   (docs/RUBRIC.md sect.2). The workspace write itself -- the file the rest of the
//   workflow consumes -- is performed by the deterministic InstallPlan tool
//   node, never by an LLM.
//
//   ALL PIPELINE STATE lives under /tmp/plan-batch/ (absolute, cwd-independent,
//   and it keeps the checked-out repo clean for the workflow steps that commit
//   afterwards). The workspace receives exactly one file on success --
//   plan.json -- and exactly one file on abort -- plan-batch-residual.md.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FOOT-GUNS RESPECTED (docs/primer.md sect.6 / docs/RUBRIC.md sect.3)
//
//   sect.3.1  No shape=diamond anywhere. Every gate is either a deterministic
//          shape=parallelogram routing on context.tool.last_line, or nothing.
//          There is no LLM judgment gate in this graph at all -- the compiler
//          is the judge and it is free, exhaustive and incorruptible.
//   sect.3.2  FAIL does not traverse plain edges. Every node that can FAIL has an
//          explicit `condition="outcome=fail"` edge: the two LLM nodes route
//          a hard failure (including a must_write= contract violation) into
//          the bounded loop at CheckAttempts, and the two deterministic gates
//          route a hard crash straight to Abort -- an attractor absorbs model
//          drift, not broken tools (docs/primer.md sect.4.7), so an infra crash
//          must not burn revision budget.
//   sect.3.4  Nothing in this graph reads $last_response. Every routing decision
//          reads either an exit code or a file. The 200-char truncation under
//          default_fidelity="summary:high" therefore cannot affect routing --
//          which is exactly why the LLM nodes are told to write a file and
//          print a short marker rather than emit multi-KB JSON as prose.
//   sect.3.5  All routing is on context.tool.last_line, never tool.output. Each
//          tool_command prints exactly one label to stdout and sends every
//          diagnostic to a file or to stderr.
//   sect.3.9  Checkpoints do not resume, so ResetPlanState is a real node: a
//          reused CI checkout carrying a stale plan.json or a stale attempt
//          counter would otherwise let a prior run's artifact sail through
//          the gate, or start this run's budget already spent (docs/RUBRIC.md sect.4,
//          idempotency -- the same bug fixed in idea_to_pr.dot's ResetFixState).
//
// TIER DISCIPLINE (docs/primer.md sect.4.1): only two nodes are LLM nodes, and both
// are there for judgment (how to cut an issue into independently verifiable
// lanes). Every other node is deterministic shell. There is deliberately no
// "Report" box node -- summarising a known outcome is typing, not judgment, so
// the terminals are parallelogram context stamps instead.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Context inputs:
//   $goal - the GitHub issue context block, supplied by amplifier-app-actions.
//
// Context outputs:
//   plan.result  - "accepted" | "aborted"
//   plan.path    - "plan.json" (relative to $GITHUB_WORKSPACE) when accepted
//   plan.residual - "plan-batch-residual.md" when aborted
//
// Required env: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (provider auth), GH_TOKEN (issue access by
// the surrounding workflow). GITHUB_WORKSPACE is provided by the runner.
//
// USAGE (remote source, no local checkout of this repo needed by the caller):
//   git+https://github.com/kenotron-ms/attractor-pipelines@main#subdirectory=pipelines/plan-batch/plan-batch.dot

digraph plan_batch {
  graph [
    label="Plan Batch -- GitHub issue to a compiler-accepted plan.json",
    goal="$goal",
    rankdir=LR,
    default_max_retry=2,
    default_fidelity="summary:high",
    // Unit-suffixed on purpose: a BARE integer here is MILLISECONDS
    // (docs/primer.md sect.6, EXTENSIONS sect.15), so "45m" is the safe spelling.
    max_pipeline_duration="45m",
    // The engine fails loud without an explicit model. Reasoning effort is
    // spent on the GENERATOR here rather than on a gate, because this
    // pipeline's gate is a deterministic compiler -- already perfect and
    // free -- so generator quality is what sets the iteration count.
    model_stylesheet="* { llm_model: claude-sonnet-4-6; llm_provider: anthropic; } #AuthorPlan { reasoning_effort: high; } #RevisePlan { reasoning_effort: high; }"
  ];

  Start [shape=Mdiamond, label="Start"];
  Exit  [shape=Msquare,  label="Exit"];

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Idempotency guard. Checkpoints do not resume (docs/RUBRIC.md sect.3.9), and a
  // GitHub Actions checkout can be reused, so a fresh run must start from a
  // clean slate: no stale plan.json that the gate would happily accept
  // without this run's planner ever succeeding, no stale attempt counter
  // that would trip the abort cap prematurely, no stale error line that
  // RevisePlan would chase.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ResetPlanState [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Reset Plan State",
    timeout="1m",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nPB_WS=${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-$PWD}\nrm -rf /tmp/plan-batch\nmkdir -p /tmp/plan-batch /tmp/plan-batch/goals\nrm -f /tmp/plan-check.dot\nrm -f \"$PB_WS/plan.json\" \"$PB_WS/plan-batch-residual.md\"\nrm -rf \"$PB_WS/plan-batch-goals\"\nprintf '0' > /tmp/plan-batch/attempts.txt\n: > /tmp/plan-batch/history.log\nprintf 'no error recorded yet' > /tmp/plan-batch/last_error.txt\nprintf 'reset'\n"
  ];

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // WORK NODE 1 -- the planner. Judgment: how to cut this issue into lanes
  // that can actually be verified by a machine. Writes ONLY to the absolute
  // path /tmp/plan-batch/plan.json; must_write= makes the engine fail the
  // node closed if that artifact is absent, stale, or whitespace-only, so a
  // node that "says" it wrote the plan but did not cannot pass this point.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  AuthorPlan [
    shape=box, fidelity="summary:high",
    label="Author plan.json from the issue",
    timeout="12m",
    must_write="/tmp/plan-batch/plan.json",
    prompt="You are the PLANNER node of a compiler-gated attractor pipeline. Turn ONE GitHub issue into a REAL-WORK goal-batch decomposition: a plan.json PLUS one goal-condition file per lane, written to fixed ABSOLUTE paths under /tmp/plan-batch/.\n\nTHE ISSUE (supplied by the GitHub Actions runner as this pipeline's goal -- it carries the event type, the issue #number, owner/repo, Title, Body, Labels and Author):\n$goal\n\nWHAT RUNS YOUR PLAN LATER (author the right thing): each lane is executed by the real-work brick subgraphs/goal_lane_impl.dot inside its own git worktree. That brick READS the lane's goal_condition_file and IMPLEMENTS the goal described there -- it does NOT invent work and it does NOT just write a marker. So every lane needs BOTH a machine-checkable verifier_argv AND a goal-condition file you author now.\n\nGROUND RULES\n- Write the plan to /tmp/plan-batch/plan.json EXACTLY, and each lane's goal file to /tmp/plan-batch/goals/<lane_id>.goal.md EXACTLY (run `mkdir -p /tmp/plan-batch/goals` first). Agent nodes in this engine do not have a dependable working directory, so these absolute paths are the only safe targets. Deterministic downstream nodes copy them into the workspace and the workflow rewrites goal_condition_file to the committed location -- do NOT try to guess run ids or workspace paths.\n- Do NOT run the compiler yourself and do NOT declare the plan valid. A deterministic gate runs `python3 -m compiler plan.json -o /tmp/plan-check.dot`; a second deterministic gate confirms every lane's goal file actually exists on disk. Their results -- not your opinion -- decide what happens next. If either rejects your work you will be handed the exact reason and asked to fix that one thing.\n- Output strict JSON only for plan.json: no comments, no trailing commas, no markdown fence.\n\nSTEP 1 -- READ THE AUTHORITATIVE SCHEMA.\nThe checked-out repository (the GITHUB_WORKSPACE env var) contains compiler/README.md, the integration contract for plan.json. Read it before writing anything:\n  cat \"${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-.}/compiler/README.md\"\nIt is the source of truth for every field, charset restriction and cross-field rule. The reminder in STEP 3 is a floor; when the two disagree, compiler/README.md wins.\n\nSTEP 2 -- DECOMPOSE THE ISSUE (goalify discipline).\nA lane is ONE independently completable unit of REAL work, sized so a single agent can finish it in one sitting. For every lane you must be able to name a command that would FAIL if that lane's work were reverted -- that command is the lane's verifier_argv.\n- verifier_argv is load-bearing. It must be a real, machine-checkable command -- a test invocation, a build, a linter, a grep for concrete content in a real file -- that exits 0 ONLY when the lane's actual deliverable exists. It runs at the repo root INSIDE the lane's git worktree, so reference repo-relative paths of the WORK (never the goal file, which lives outside the worktree). A self-report is not a verifier. If you cannot name such a command, re-cut the lane. NO-HUMAN / BROWSER RULE: never author a check that needs a person -- the words 'manual', 'verify manually', 'manually confirm', 'by hand', or any human-in-the-loop / human-gate step are FORBIDDEN in verifier_argv and in the goal file, because the runner executes unattended and a human gate fails the lane closed. When a lane's deliverable is a runnable web UI (a web app, SPA, or frontend), the Done-when check MUST be a headless browser assertion: build the app, start its server, run an end-to-end Playwright test (`npx playwright test <spec>`, headless, exit 0 only when the UI genuinely works), then stop the server -- the runner provides Node and Chromium (install the matching browser with `npx playwright install chromium`; OS deps are already present). If the issue or scenario names its own start_cmd / url / e2e_runner, use those.\n- Lanes that can proceed at the same time share a wave. A lane needing another lane's output belongs to a LATER wave and must name that lane in depends_on. Every depends_on entry must name a real lane in an earlier-or-equal wave that also precedes this lane in integration_order (the compiler enforces this).\n- integration_order is a permutation of ALL lane ids, non-decreasing by wave.\n- Aim for 2-4 genuinely-independent lanes. One lane means this issue did not need a batch -- say so in description and still emit the single lane. Prefer FEWER solid lanes over many fragile ones. If the issue is large, cut the smallest coherent slice that stands on its own and state in description what you deferred.\n- marker_file / marker_content are the lane's completion marker: a path under artifacts/ named after the lane, content of the form <lane_id>:ok.\n\nSTEP 3 -- REQUIRED SHAPE (floor; compiler/README.md is authoritative).\nTop level: plan_id, lanes, waves, integration_order, terminals.\n- plan_id: derive from the issue number in $goal as issue_<n> (e.g. issue_412). If no issue number, derive from the title. MUST match ^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$ -- letters, digits, underscores only, NO hyphens, not starting with a digit.\n- lanes: an object mapping lane_id -> an object with EXACTLY these fields:\n    \"wave\": <int >= 1>,\n    \"depends_on\": [<lane ids>],\n    \"child_dot\": \"subgraphs/goal_lane_impl.dot\",            (REQUIRED -- this exact value; the real-work brick)\n    \"goal_condition_file\": \"goals/<lane_id>.goal.md\",        (REQUIRED -- this exact relative form; the workflow rewrites it to the committed absolute path)\n    \"verifier_argv\": [<argv strings>, non-empty],\n    \"marker_file\": \"artifacts/<lane_id>.done\",\n    \"marker_content\": \"<lane_id>:ok\"\n  Lane ids must match ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]{0,63}$ and MUST equal the <lane_id> stem in goals/<lane_id>.goal.md.\n- waves: [{\"wave\": 1}, {\"wave\": 2}, ...] -- strictly increasing, unique, every declared wave has >= 1 lane. Optional per-wave \"concurrency\".\n- integration_order: [<every lane id exactly once>], non-decreasing by wave.\n- terminals: [\"COMPLETE\", \"RESIDUALS_READY\", \"INFRA_FAILURE\", \"ABORTED\"].\nAlso set a human-readable description, and budgets with realistic values (smoke-fixture defaults are far too small): { \"max_adaptive_attempts_per_lane\": 3, \"lane_wall_timeout_seconds\": 3600, \"verifier_timeout_seconds\": 300 }.\n\nSTEP 4 -- AUTHOR ONE GOAL-CONDITION FILE PER LANE.\nFor each lane, write /tmp/plan-batch/goals/<lane_id>.goal.md -- a /goal-style stop condition the real-work brick will read and implement. Each file MUST contain, in plain markdown:\n  # Lane <lane_id>\n  ## Outcome -- the concrete change to make true in the repository (repo-relative paths, specific files/functions).\n  ## Steps -- what to implement, concretely enough that an agent can do it without re-reading the issue.\n  ## Done when -- the machine check that proves it: state the SAME check your verifier_argv runs, and that it must exit 0.\n  ## Final step (REQUIRED) -- after the work is done and the check passes, write the file <marker_file> (the lane's marker_file) containing exactly <marker_content> and nothing else. Explain that this marker is how the batch orchestrator confirms the lane finished, so it must be the LAST action.\nKeep each goal file self-contained and consistent with that lane's verifier_argv, marker_file and marker_content. Do NOT reference other lanes' work except through declared depends_on.\n\nSTEP 5 -- WRITE AND STOP.\nWrite plan.json to /tmp/plan-batch/plan.json and each goal file to /tmp/plan-batch/goals/<lane_id>.goal.md. Verify with `cat` that plan.json and every goal file on disk are complete, and that the set of goals/<lane_id>.goal.md files EXACTLY matches the lanes in plan.json. Do not narrate at length -- the files are the deliverable. Print the single line: plan_authored"
  ];

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The workspace write. Deterministic on purpose: this is the file the rest
  // of the workflow consumes, so no LLM is allowed to place it. Also
  // pre-checks JSON parseability, because `python3 -m compiler` on
  // unparseable JSON raises a raw JSONDecodeError traceback (exit 1) instead
  // of the clean `error: invalid plan spec: ...` line the loop feeds on --
  // catching it here keeps the feedback channel curated and named.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  InstallPlan [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Install plan.json into the workspace",
    timeout="2m",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nPB_WS=${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-$PWD}\nSRC=/tmp/plan-batch/plan.json\nmkdir -p /tmp/plan-batch\nATT=$(cat /tmp/plan-batch/attempts.txt 2>/dev/null || printf '0')\ncase \"$ATT\" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ATT=0 ;; esac\nATT=$((ATT + 1))\nfail() {\n  printf '%s\\n' \"$1\" > /tmp/plan-batch/last_error.txt\n  printf 'attempt %s: %s\\n' \"$ATT\" \"$1\" >> /tmp/plan-batch/history.log\n  printf 'install_failed'\n  exit 0\n}\nif [ ! -s \"$SRC\" ]; then\n  fail 'The previous attempt produced no artifact: /tmp/plan-batch/plan.json is missing or empty. Author the plan from scratch and write it to that exact absolute path.'\nfi\nif ! python3 -c \"import json,sys; json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))\" \"$SRC\" 2> /tmp/plan-batch/json_error.txt; then\n  fail \"The plan is not valid JSON. The Python parser said: $(tail -1 /tmp/plan-batch/json_error.txt)\"\nfi\nif ! cp \"$SRC\" \"$PB_WS/plan.json\"; then\n  fail \"Could not copy $SRC to $PB_WS/plan.json.\"\nfi\nprintf 'installed'\n"
  ];

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // THE GATE. The whole pipeline exists to make this node print plan_ok.
  //
  // It is deliberately stricter than "exit 0": the compiler writes nothing on
  // rejection, so a stale /tmp/plan-check.dot from a previous attempt could
  // otherwise look like fresh evidence -- it is removed first, then required
  // to exist non-empty alongside plan.json before plan_ok is printed. This is
  // the independent verification docs/RUBRIC.md sect.2 demands, applied to the one
  // claim that matters here.
  //
  // On rejection it records ONE curated line (the compiler's own named
  // reason) for RevisePlan to act on, plus an append-only history.log that
  // exists solely so RevisePlan can tell whether the loop is contracting or
  // oscillating (docs/primer.md sect.4.9). The curated single line -- not the log --
  // is what drives the next attempt, so the feedback channel cannot silt up.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  CompilerGate [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Compiler Gate (python3 -m compiler)",
    timeout="5m",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nPB_WS=${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-$PWD}\nmkdir -p /tmp/plan-batch\nATT=$(cat /tmp/plan-batch/attempts.txt 2>/dev/null || printf '0')\ncase \"$ATT\" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ATT=0 ;; esac\nATT=$((ATT + 1))\nrm -f /tmp/plan-check.dot\nif ! cd \"$PB_WS\"; then\n  printf 'Could not enter the workspace directory %s.\\n' \"$PB_WS\" > /tmp/plan-batch/last_error.txt\n  printf 'plan_rejected'\n  exit 0\nfi\nreason=''\nif python3 -m compiler plan.json -o /tmp/plan-check.dot > /tmp/plan-batch/compiler_stdout.txt 2> /tmp/plan-batch/compiler_stderr.txt; then\n  if [ -s /tmp/plan-check.dot ] && [ -s plan.json ]; then\n    printf 'plan_ok'\n    exit 0\n  fi\n  reason='The compiler exited 0 but produced no non-empty parent .dot (or plan.json vanished) -- treat the plan as rejected.'\nfi\nif [ -z \"$reason\" ]; then\n  reason=$(grep -m1 '^error: invalid plan spec:' /tmp/plan-batch/compiler_stderr.txt 2>/dev/null)\nfi\nif [ -z \"$reason\" ]; then\n  reason=\"The compiler failed without emitting a named plan-spec error. Last stderr: $(tail -3 /tmp/plan-batch/compiler_stderr.txt 2>/dev/null | tr '\\n' ' ')\"\nfi\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$reason\" > /tmp/plan-batch/last_error.txt\nprintf 'attempt %s: %s\\n' \"$ATT\" \"$reason\" >> /tmp/plan-batch/history.log\nprintf 'plan_rejected'\n"
  ];

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The bound, and it is a DECISION POINT, not a fuse (docs/primer.md sect.4.5).
  // Counts authoring attempts, not engine retries: default_max_retry only
  // bounds a single node's own execution attempts, it does not limit how
  // many times the graph traverses a back-edge. Reaching the cap routes to
  // Abort, which produces a readable residual note -- not a silent exit and
  // not a bare FAIL.
  //
  // Budget: attempts.txt starts at 0, so 1 initial authoring pass plus 3
  // revisions = 4 authoring attempts before the cap trips.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  CheckAttempts [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Check Attempts (bounded decision)",
    timeout="1m",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nmkdir -p /tmp/plan-batch\ncount=$(cat /tmp/plan-batch/attempts.txt 2>/dev/null || printf '0')\ncase \"$count\" in ''|*[!0-9]*) count=0 ;; esac\ncount=$((count + 1))\nprintf '%s' \"$count\" > /tmp/plan-batch/attempts.txt\nmax=${plan_max_attempts:-4}\nif [ \"$count\" -lt \"$max\" ]; then\n  printf 'revise_ok'\nelse\n  printf 'attempts_exhausted'\nfi\n"
  ];

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // WORK NODE 2 -- the repair pass. Separate from AuthorPlan on purpose: a
  // retry that re-runs the original prompt is a coin re-flip, same
  // distribution, new sample (docs/primer.md sect.4.4). This node is prompted
  // against the compiler's own named error and is told to change ONLY what
  // that error requires -- and, if the history shows the same rejection
  // twice, to stop patching the symptom and re-cut the decomposition
  // (docs/primer.md sect.4.7: an attractor absorbs model drift, not a wrong plan
  // shape).
  //
  // Same must_write= contract as AuthorPlan: a repair pass that does not
  // actually rewrite the artifact fails closed instead of silently
  // re-submitting the previous attempt to the gate.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  RevisePlan [
    shape=box, fidelity="summary:high",
    label="Revise plan.json (compiler-named error)",
    timeout="10m",
    must_write="/tmp/plan-batch/plan.json",
    prompt="You are the REPAIR node of a compiler-gated attractor pipeline. Your previous attempt (a plan.json PLUS one goal-condition file per lane) did not survive a deterministic gate. Fix exactly what was named -- nothing else.\n\nTHE ISSUE (unchanged; still the thing being planned):\n$goal\n\nSTEP 1 -- READ THE RECORDED REJECTION.\n  cat /tmp/plan-batch/last_error.txt\nThat file holds ONE curated line describing why the last attempt did not pass. Then read the attempt history:\n  cat /tmp/plan-batch/history.log\n\nSTEP 2 -- READ THE CURRENT ATTEMPT (files may be missing):\n  cat /tmp/plan-batch/plan.json\n  ls /tmp/plan-batch/goals/ 2>/dev/null && cat /tmp/plan-batch/goals/*.goal.md 2>/dev/null\n\nSTEP 3 -- HANDLE WHICHEVER CASE APPLIES.\n(a) No artifact / plan.json missing or empty -> author the plan from scratch against `cat \"${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-.}/compiler/README.md\"` and write it to /tmp/plan-batch/plan.json (and its goal files, per case (d)).\n(b) Not valid JSON -> the content is malformed, not the design. Re-emit the same decomposition as strict JSON: no comments, no trailing commas, no markdown fence, no truncation.\n(c) `error: invalid plan spec:` -> the compiler named a specific field/lane/cross-field rule. Read that exact rule in compiler/README.md and change ONLY what it requires. Do not redesign lanes that were not implicated.\n(d) A goal-condition file is missing/empty, or a lane is not a real-work lane -> EVERY lane MUST set \"child_dot\": \"subgraphs/goal_lane_impl.dot\" and \"goal_condition_file\": \"goals/<lane_id>.goal.md\", and you MUST have written /tmp/plan-batch/goals/<lane_id>.goal.md for EVERY lane. Add the missing child_dot / goal_condition_file fields and/or write the missing goal file(s). The set of goals/<lane_id>.goal.md files must exactly match the lanes in plan.json. Each goal file states the concrete real work, the machine check matching verifier_argv, and a REQUIRED final step that writes the lane's marker_file with its marker_content. NO-HUMAN / BROWSER RULE (applies here too): never author a check that needs a person -- 'manual', 'verify manually', 'manually confirm', 'by hand', or any human-in-the-loop / human-gate step are FORBIDDEN in verifier_argv and the goal file (the runner is unattended; a human gate fails the lane closed). For a runnable web UI, the Done-when check MUST be a headless browser assertion: build, start the server, run `npx playwright test <spec>` (headless, exit 0 only when the UI genuinely works), then stop the server -- the runner provides Node and Chromium (`npx playwright install chromium`; OS deps already present). Use the issue/scenario's own start_cmd / url / e2e_runner when named.\n\nSTEP 4 -- WATCH THE REGIME.\nIf history.log shows you already attempted this same repair and it was rejected again, stop patching the symptom: the DECOMPOSITION is wrong, not the syntax. Re-cut lanes/waves/depends_on/integration_order so the violated constraint is satisfied by construction -- most commonly by moving a lane to a later wave, or reordering integration_order so every dependency precedes its dependent.\n\nCONSTRAINTS MOST OFTEN THE CULPRIT:\n- plan_id must match ^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$ (no hyphens, no leading digit).\n- Every declared wave must have at least one lane; an empty wave is rejected.\n- integration_order must be a permutation of ALL lane ids, non-decreasing by wave.\n- Every depends_on entry names a real lane in an earlier-or-equal wave that also precedes this lane in integration_order.\n- verifier_argv present and non-empty for every lane; it checks the real deliverable at the repo root.\n- EVERY lane sets child_dot=subgraphs/goal_lane_impl.dot and goal_condition_file=goals/<lane_id>.goal.md, and a matching non-empty /tmp/plan-batch/goals/<lane_id>.goal.md exists.\n- terminals present and non-empty.\n\nSTEP 5 -- WRITE AND STOP.\nWrite the corrected plan to /tmp/plan-batch/plan.json (overwrite) and any missing/edited goal files to /tmp/plan-batch/goals/<lane_id>.goal.md. Confirm with `cat` that plan.json and every referenced goal file are complete on disk. Do not run the compiler and do not claim the plan is valid -- the gates decide. Print the single line: plan_revised"
  ];

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // THE GOAL-FILE GATE (real-work completeness -- RUBRIC.md sect.2).
  //
  // A compiler-valid plan is necessary but NOT sufficient for a real-work
  // batch. This deterministic gate independently confirms the real state the
  // downstream execute-plan run depends on: (1) EVERY lane is a real-work lane
  // (child_dot=subgraphs/goal_lane_impl.dot), and (2) EVERY lane's authored
  // goal-condition file actually exists non-empty on disk. It reads plan.json
  // itself (never an LLM's say-so) and copies the goal files into the
  // workspace staging dir plan-batch-goals/ for the workflow to relocate and
  // commit. A missing goal file or a fixture-brick lane loops back through
  // the SAME bounded CheckAttempts decision as any other planner miss -- a
  // real-work plan that forgot its instructions must not be called accepted.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  InstallGoals [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Install Goal Files (real-work gate)",
    timeout="2m",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nPB_WS=${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-$PWD}\nSRC=/tmp/plan-batch/plan.json\nGDIR=/tmp/plan-batch/goals\nSTAGE=\"$PB_WS/plan-batch-goals\"\nmkdir -p /tmp/plan-batch \"$GDIR\"\nATT=$(cat /tmp/plan-batch/attempts.txt 2>/dev/null || printf '0')\ncase \"$ATT\" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ATT=0 ;; esac\nATT=$((ATT + 1))\nfail() {\n  printf '%s\\n' \"$1\" > /tmp/plan-batch/last_error.txt\n  printf 'attempt %s: %s\\n' \"$ATT\" \"$1\" >> /tmp/plan-batch/history.log\n  printf 'goals_missing'\n  exit 0\n}\nif ! python3 - \"$SRC\" > /tmp/plan-batch/expected_goals.txt 2> /tmp/plan-batch/goals_error.txt <<'PYEOF'\nimport json,sys,os\nplan=json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))\nlanes=plan.get('lanes',{})\nbad=[]; miss=[]\nfor lid,l in sorted(lanes.items()):\n    if l.get('child_dot') != 'subgraphs/goal_lane_impl.dot':\n        bad.append(lid)\n    gcf=l.get('goal_condition_file')\n    if not gcf:\n        miss.append(lid)\n    else:\n        print(os.path.basename(gcf))\nerrs=[]\nif bad: errs.append('lanes not using child_dot=subgraphs/goal_lane_impl.dot: '+','.join(bad))\nif miss: errs.append('lanes missing goal_condition_file: '+','.join(miss))\nif errs:\n    sys.stderr.write('; '.join(errs)); sys.exit(3)\nPYEOF\nthen\n  fail \"Every lane must be a real-work lane: set child_dot=subgraphs/goal_lane_impl.dot and goal_condition_file=goals/<lane_id>.goal.md. $(cat /tmp/plan-batch/goals_error.txt 2>/dev/null)\"\nfi\nrm -rf \"$STAGE\"\nmkdir -p \"$STAGE\"\nwhile IFS= read -r base; do\n  [ -n \"$base\" ] || continue\n  if [ ! -s \"$GDIR/$base\" ]; then\n    fail \"The goal-condition file '$base' referenced by plan.json was not authored to $GDIR/$base (missing or empty). Author every lane's goal file to /tmp/plan-batch/goals/<lane_id>.goal.md.\"\n  fi\n  cp \"$GDIR/$base\" \"$STAGE/$base\" || fail \"Could not copy $GDIR/$base into the workspace staging dir $STAGE.\"\ndone < /tmp/plan-batch/expected_goals.txt\nprintf 'goals_ok'\n"
  ];

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // TERMINAL: converged. Reachable only through CompilerGate's plan_ok line,
  // which already required compiler exit 0 plus real artifacts on disk. This
  // node does no verification of its own -- it is a deterministic context
  // stamp, which is why it is a parallelogram and not a box (an LLM here
  // would be typing, not judging).
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  MarkAccepted [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Mark Accepted (compiler-verified)",
    timeout="1m",
    tool_command="printf '{\"plan.result\": \"accepted\", \"plan.path\": \"plan.json\", \"plan.goals_dir\": \"plan-batch-goals\"}'",
    parse_json="true"
  ];

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // TERMINAL: budget exhausted, or a deterministic gate crashed outright.
  //
  // Removing plan.json is the point of this node. A rejected plan left in the
  // workspace is worse than no plan: the consuming workflow relocates and
  // publishes whatever plan.json it finds, so a malformed one would be
  // shipped by a job that "completed". Deleting it makes the absence of
  // plan.json the workflow's own evidence-based gate, and the residual note
  // explains -- with the compiler's real words and the full attempt history
  // -- what went wrong, so a human gets a diagnosis rather than a shrug.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  Abort [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Abort (residual note, no plan.json)",
    timeout="2m",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nPB_WS=${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-$PWD}\nattempts=$(cat /tmp/plan-batch/attempts.txt 2>/dev/null || printf '?')\nreason=$(cat /tmp/plan-batch/last_error.txt 2>/dev/null || printf 'no compiler error was recorded')\nrm -f \"$PB_WS/plan.json\"\nrm -rf \"$PB_WS/plan-batch-goals\"\n{\n  printf '# plan-batch: no compiler-valid plan.json was produced\\n\\n'\n  printf 'The planner made %s authoring attempt(s). The deterministic gate\\n' \"$attempts\"\n  printf '(python3 -m compiler plan.json -o /tmp/plan-check.dot) rejected every one,\\n'\n  printf 'or failed outright, so this run produced NO plan.json. That absence is\\n'\n  printf 'intentional: a plan the compiler rejects must not be shipped downstream.\\n\\n'\n  printf 'Last recorded reason:\\n\\n    %s\\n\\n' \"$reason\"\n  printf 'Attempt history:\\n\\n'\n  sed 's/^/    /' /tmp/plan-batch/history.log 2>/dev/null\n  printf '\\nNext steps: clarify the issue so the lanes can be cut with real\\n'\n  printf 'machine-checkable verifiers, or hand-author a plan.json against\\n'\n  printf 'compiler/README.md and re-run.\\n'\n} > \"$PB_WS/plan-batch-residual.md\" 2>/dev/null || true\nprintf 'aborted'\nexit 0\n"
  ];

  MarkAborted [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Mark Aborted",
    timeout="1m",
    tool_command="printf '{\"plan.result\": \"aborted\", \"plan.residual\": \"plan-batch-residual.md\"}'",
    parse_json="true"
  ];

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════ EDGES ═══════════════════════════

  Start          -> ResetPlanState;
  ResetPlanState -> AuthorPlan;

  AuthorPlan     -> InstallPlan;
  // FAIL does not traverse the plain edge above (docs/RUBRIC.md sect.3.2). A hard
  // failure here -- including a must_write= contract violation after retries
  // -- is model failure, so it enters the bounded loop rather than dead-ending.
  AuthorPlan     -> CheckAttempts [condition="outcome=fail", label="no artifact / node failed"];

  InstallPlan    -> CompilerGate  [condition="context.tool.last_line=installed", weight="2", label="installed"];
  InstallPlan    -> CheckAttempts [condition="context.tool.last_line=install_failed", label="unusable artifact"];
  // The tool itself crashed (no python3, unwritable workspace): infrastructure,
  // not model drift -- do not spend revision budget on it (docs/primer.md sect.4.7).
  InstallPlan    -> Abort         [condition="outcome=fail", label="infra failure"];

  // ── THE GATE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  CompilerGate   -> InstallGoals  [condition="context.tool.last_line=plan_ok", weight="2", label="compiler accepted"];
  CompilerGate   -> CheckAttempts [condition="context.tool.last_line=plan_rejected", label="compiler rejected"];
  CompilerGate   -> Abort         [condition="outcome=fail", label="infra failure"];

  // ── THE GOAL-FILE GATE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  InstallGoals   -> MarkAccepted  [condition="context.tool.last_line=goals_ok", weight="2", label="goal files present"];
  InstallGoals   -> CheckAttempts [condition="context.tool.last_line=goals_missing", label="goal file missing / not real-work"];
  InstallGoals   -> Abort         [condition="outcome=fail", label="infra failure"];

  // ── THE BOUND ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  CheckAttempts  -> RevisePlan    [condition="context.tool.last_line=revise_ok", label="revise (max $plan_max_attempts, default 4)"];
  CheckAttempts  -> Abort         [condition="context.tool.last_line=attempts_exhausted", label="budget spent -- escalate"];
  CheckAttempts  -> Abort         [condition="outcome=fail", label="infra failure"];

  // ── THE LOOP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  RevisePlan     -> InstallPlan   [loop_restart="true", label="re-submit to the gate"];
  RevisePlan     -> CheckAttempts [condition="outcome=fail", label="no artifact / node failed"];

  MarkAccepted   -> Exit;
  Abort          -> MarkAborted;
  MarkAborted    -> Exit;
}
